Harness slips this round (results right, the check misread):
- Conversation cache: the harness said FAIL only because its "reads <= 50 % of the prompt" test tripped on a turn whose new
  message is over half its prompt (5,006 of 9,210). Every turn reused the previous prompt and read exactly the new text;
  back to chat A from RAM 571 tokens read in 6.69 s, from the disk slot after a restart 554 in 6.88 s (fresh 49.17 s).
- C.10 thinking: the summary read the wrong run's client output; the numbers above are from the right one.
- Exact continuations: 4 hits / 0 misses but no pair identical in one engine (fresh engines are identical). Something
  carried over between requests changes the path - the fixed-seed pairs in section 6 rule out the reuse path; the
  sampler counter (never reset per request, ds4_main.cpp:327) is part of it, not all of it.

Swap during the round: x5-B grew swap 437 -> 929 MB and x5-A to 979 MB (engine memory never swapped, 767 swap-in pages
in 19 min, no streak). Under the guard installed 10-26 08:11 (below) both x5 runs would count as "failed: memory"
(later swap-out bursts); they were report-only runs, so nothing that ships depends on them.

## 2. Maya v1.1.28 head-to-head - Maya FAILED on this PC

| Arm | decode | P4K | P20K | GB read per 20K prompt | Result |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S1 Strata (ship config) | 27.64 | 308.2 | 514.1 | 172.3 | clean |
| M1 Maya (default, in place) | - | 399.2 | 693.2 | 136.1 | failed: its own memory swapped (1,089 MB) |
| M2 Maya (its own tuned) | - | 480.5 | 899.5 | 88.5 | failed: its own memory swapped (544 MB) |
| Mcheck Maya (copy capped at 50 GB) | - | 377.6 | 688.9 | 116.3 | failed: its own memory swapped (163 MB) |

Maya has no store like Strata's O_DIRECT RAM store, only `--mmap-experts on/off/auto` and a static copy (DS4_COPY_GB).
In every mode the kernel swapped Maya's engine memory while it read experts through the page cache, with ~34 GB still
free - which points to swappiness (vm.swappiness = 60, read only, not changed), not a real RAM shortage. Maya is a
fallback only; Karl may lower swappiness later and retry the 10-minute check.

## 3. Ports into v0.2.6

### #343 - prompt skip across the layer split: KEPT
- What: on a long prompt the later layers (20-39, all on the 5080) compute only the last 2,668 rows; the early rows go to
  the KV cache only. Our port lets the skip run with a layer split (the skip layer and all later layers on one GPU).
- Engine line: "prompt skip: layers 20-39 from CUDA1, 9332 early rows KV-only, 2668 kept".
- Rounding check: KL mean 0.00331, p90 0.00633, KL>1 0, top-1 2023 vs references 0.00663 / 0.01278 / 1 / 2010 and
  0.00620 / 0.01401 / 1 / 2012 -> PASS.
- Re-judged under the 16:55 rule (five checks): PASS, **narrowly on the text-NLL check** - NLL 0.6672 vs the ship
  config's 0.6624, +0.0048 against a bound of 0.0049 (1.10 x 0.0045, the larger reference's distance; margin 0.00013).
  Changed top-1 picks 17 (bound 33). Against stock v0.2.6, the final config with #343 and 8/32 is +0.0013 (bound 0.0111).
- A/B/A (3 passes): B decode 28.19, P4K 332.7, P20K 600.5, 110 GB read per 20K prompt.
  vs A2: decode +2.7 % (noise 0.7), P4K +6.6 %, P20K +16.2 %; vs A3: +2.9 % (0.7), +8.0 %, +16.8 %. A1 failed the swap
  rule (a second swap-out burst of 4 MB) and was replaced by A3.

### BORROW - dropped this round (the next round's main item)
- Its layout marker is the one Maya uses for every GGUF in-place expert layout; porting it means re-laying out the expert
  slots, the tail region and the cache (~1.5-2 days). Karl: drop it now, make it the next DeepSeek round's main item.
- Strata upstream may port it first (Niko has been pulling Maya changes within days): the Saturday release check watches
  for it.

### IQ_Q8K - GPU experts' activations rounded like the CPU lane's: DROPPED
- What: Maya's change rounds the low-bit experts' activations on the GPU the way the CPU lane does (Q8_K, per 256 values)
  instead of q8_1 (per 32), so an expert gives the same sums wherever placement or the cache puts it.
- Maya's own evidence: KL against FP8 0.162 with Q8_K vs 0.171 without.
- Where it acts in Strata: only decode (one token at a time) and windows of up to 8 tokens (DSpark verify windows, the
  async expert path, DS4_ASYNC_T = 8). Prompts of 96 tokens and up take the prompt path (F32 / F16 activations, no int8
  rounding), and windows of 9-16 tokens take a synchronous path through generic matrix-vector kernels. So the usual gate
  (128-token windows) and the first re-test (16-token windows) both gave output byte-identical to stock - they never
  reached it. The test that counts runs in 8-token windows.
- Rounding gate (8-token windows, short8, 16,376 positions): PASS - mean 0.01088, p90 0.01679, KL>1 18, top-1 16,015 vs
  references 0.00848 / 0.01544 / 9 / 16,048 (4-token windows) and 0.01064 / 0.01811 / 19 / 16,008 (CPU share). Switched
  off (DS4_IQ_Q8K=0) it is byte-identical to stock.
- Placement test (the pinpoint steps with and without it, KL against the step before, mean / p90): expert profile
  0.00888 / 0.01615 -> 0.00870 / 0.01542; order + split 0.01045 / 0.01753 -> 0.00888 / 0.01670; decay 0.00638 /
  0.00929 -> 0.00566 / 0.00844. 2-15 % less, not the halving the bar asked for.
- Decode check (Karl, before dropping it): 3 prompts (106-123 tokens), 100 tokens each, seed 4242, Strata's stock
  profile vs ours, IQ_Q8K on and off; same-placement controls 100 of 100 both ways. Tokens matching before the first
  divergence (p1 / p2 / p3): as served - with 3 / 24 / 29, without 29 / 24 / 29; prompt in 8-token windows (report only)
  - with 45 / 25 / 20, without 27 / 20 / 36. No prompt matches throughout either way; IQ_Q8K does not raise the count.
- Verdict: dropped (Karl's rule); the combined A/B/A was not run. Reading: on this engine the GPU/CPU activation
  rounding is a small part of why placement changes the answers - the prompt path (F32 / F16 on the GPU, Q8_K on the CPU
  lane), summation order and batching move them as much. Maya's FP8 comparison (0.162 with Q8_K vs 0.171) is a
  quality-vs-reference gain, not a consistency one, and our gate shows IQ_Q8K within rounding of stock - consistent.
- Lesson (cost one invalid run): the engine rewrites its --expert-profile file at exit (CLI decode and serve modes);
  every run must get its own copy. Strata's stock profile file was rewritten for ~10 minutes and restored from git.

### Strata + #343 vs Maya
- Strata+#343: P4K 332.7, P20K 600.5, 110 GB per 20K prompt. Maya M1: 399.2 / 693.2 / 136 GB; M2: 480.5 / 899.5 / 88.5 GB.
- Maya M1 reads more than Strata+#343 but runs 15 % faster on 20K prompts (~4.9 vs ~3.4 GB/s effective): Strata is no
  longer drive-bound - the 4060 Ti sets the pace (per 20K prompt it streams 76 GB over its PCIe x4 link, 40 GB of it
  from the drive at ~1 GB/s, and waits 9.4 s of 38 s).
- How much of the gap BORROW and bigger chunks explain: none of the measured gap - Maya ran with neither. What they would
  add on top for Strata is the next round's question (BORROW: more VRAM experts on both cards, fewer bytes over the x4
  link).

### Re-rank of Maya's prompt-compute changes (the 4060 Ti is the pace now)
1. Fewer layers on the 4060 Ti (8/32): P20K +10-20 % estimated, zero code - screened and KEPT this round (P20K +16 %,
   section 4).
2. Earlier / larger read share for the 4060 Ti's pipeline (Strata-side): +10-15 % if its 9.4 s of waits halve.
3. Tensor-core prompt attention (ATTN_MMA): +5-8 %.
4. Leaner expert tiles + FP16 rows + router rows: +3-6 %, exact.
5. Wider attention slabs: ~0 on an 8 GB card.
6. FAT_ROWS: <= 1 %.
#360 (copies during the routing wait) is already in v0.2.6. Details: maya-catchup.md.

