## 6. The rest of the block

- **The upstream one-line follow-up fix** (`if (!continued) m.pin();`, built offline on v0.2.6, slot keep off): 12 of 12
  follow-ups read only the last answer plus the new message - 6 / 6 after a two-pass answer (read 141 = needed, first
  token 2.9 s; stock: 0 / 6, ~7.7K re-read, 19.5 s), 6 / 6 after a one-pass answer. The issue draft (pr-drafts/47) now
  says built and tested; not posted.
- **Repeatability with a fixed seed** (four request pairs, seed 4242, expert placement frozen, `--dspark auto` as shipped):
  - stock v0.2.6 with STRATA_DS4_REUSE=0: no pair identical -> the reuse path is not the cause.
  - with the sampler counter reset per seeded request (a test build; the code never resets it): still no pair identical
    with reuse off, though the pairs come much closer (the same length and tool calls); with reuse on, 1 of 4 identical.
  - the first request of every fresh engine is identical across all three runs.
  - So the counter is part of it and something else carries over between requests. DSpark is not it: with `auto` the
    engine never loads it in this config ("routed experts 132 GB ... far more than the GPU holds (23 GB)", in every
    arm), and with `--dspark none` (10-27 01:47, the same test) still no pair is identical.
  - Carry-over (10-27; one engine scores the same 2,047-token segment twice): with the expert cache on, the second copy
    differs at both window 128 (the prompt path) and window 1 (decode's path), mostly over the first 16 positions (KL
    mean 0.28 / 0.36 there; past position 512 only 0.0010 / 0.0008, against the gate's rounding references
    0.006-0.007). **With placement frozen, window 1 is identical (2,047 / 2,047) and window 128 still differs** (KL mean
    0.0022, 15 top-1 changes). So decode is exact once placement is fixed, and something on the prompt path carries
    over between requests. The pairs' prompts (113 and 688 tokens) take that path, which fits. Candidates, not tested:
    the RAM store's contents (they keep changing between requests even with VRAM placement frozen) or the prompt pass's
    learned timing. Next test: window 128, frozen, with the RAM store off.
- **Resumed vs fresh** (teacher-forced scoring, test build, final config; re-judged 20:40 under the final five-check
  rule): all three variants are within the rounding references. Resuming itself is exact: a 4-piece resume scores
  byte-identically to the same 4 pieces without the set-aside and take-back (KL 0, 2,040 / 2,040 top-1), and a 1-piece
  resume is identical to the fresh run. The only difference is the 4-piece split's own rounding: 25 changed top-1 picks
  (bound 33), text NLL 0.6599 vs fresh 0.6624 (lower), mean KL 0.00680 / p90 0.01314 (references 0.00663 / 0.01278 and
  0.00620 / 0.01401), KL > 1: 1 (references 1 / 1). The test build's fresh scores are byte-identical to the round's
  engine run. -> results/ds41/after/resume-kl-rejudged.json
- **Context size** (report only; final config, daily store; decode and prompt speed measured on short prompts, so this is
  the cost of reserving the context, not of filling it):

  | --max-context | decode tok/s | P4K | P20K | VRAM experts (4060 Ti / 5080) | KV caches (5080) | lowest MemAvailable |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | 32K (daily) | 28.34 | 348.9 | 698.9 | 460 / 870 | 0.03 GB | 19.4 GB |
  | 64K | 28.47 | 349.9 | 695.2 | 456 / 864 | 0.05 GB | 19.4 GB |
  | 128K | 28.27 | 350.1 | 699.0 | 451 / 857 | 0.10 GB | 19.4 GB |

  128K costs 22 of 1,330 VRAM expert slots (-1.7 %) and nothing measurable in speed or RAM; no swap events, kernel log
  clean. Karl then asked for 128K if a filled-context check passes: it did, and the daily config is 128K (section 10).

## 7. Swap guard history
- Through 10-25: a run failed on a swap-in streak or engine swap; one-off growth was logged.
- 10-26 07:10 (the user): fail if swap-ins or swap-outs keep happening or any engine memory is swapped; a single push-out
  of other programs' idle pages is logged only.
- 10-26 08:11 (Karl), installed: a swap-in streak fails a run only with >= 4 MB of swap-ins in it; a swap-out burst over
  4 MB fails a run (the first burst is logged only); any swapped engine memory fails a run.
- Replayed over the round's 72 swap logs: x5-A and x5-B fail, the other 70 pass. Runs failed under it since: h2h S1's
  first try (a later 12 MB burst), Maya M1 / M2 / Mcheck (engine swap), #343's A1 (a second 4 MB burst; replaced).
- About 1.7-2.0 GB stays in swap from the idle desktop, the web UI and Chrome; swappiness 60.

## 8. Daily launcher

`./switch-model.sh deepseek` -> configs/DS41-daily.json (port 8086; backup of the 32K version:
DS41-daily.json.before-128k):
- engine `engine-v026p343-slotram-ds41` (v0.2.6 + #343 + the slot RAM cap), `--max-context 131072`, 6 experts, chunk
  8192, `--ssd-cache 68`, the final settings (4060 Ti first, 8 / 32, decay 0.8, settle 0, Engram cache 16 MB,
  STRATA_DS4_PROMPT_SKIP=1, store floor 14 GB).
- Slot keep on: up to 16 conversations in RAM, at most 1.7 GiB of them (STRATA_DS4_SLOT_RAM_GB); past either the least
  recently used goes to the folder (2 GiB, LRU).
- Its own learning profile (logs/ds41/daily-profile.bin), seeded once from the pinned seed (sha 8db93e48...).
- need_ram_mib 81,305 (unchanged: the 128K run's own figure, 77,600 with the cap, is lower); need_vram_mib 7,000.
- Effort default: Karl decides (placeholder medium; suggested 50 from sections 5 and 9).
- Launcher smoke test on 8086 after the switch: ok (10-27 02:13).

## 9. Hard set (29 items, 10-26 night): DeepSeek at its best vs Qwen at its best

29 items: the 19 that Qwen failed or passed inconsistently on 10-15 to 10-17, plus the 10 hardest by the mean grade over
every graded label (math, coding with hidden tests, tool use, multi-step); no long context. One run each. Every answer
got the same room: max_tokens 30,000, no separate thinking cap (both servers keep a quarter for the answer, so the
thinking stops at ~22,500). Grades: the suite's checkers, research also blind. Full tables: results/ds41/hard/REPORT.md.

| setting | total (mean grade x 100) | mean s per answer | mean thinking tokens | thinking at the room limit | cut off |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Qwen medium | **88.5** | 23.2 | 3,348 | 2 | 0 |
| Qwen xhigh | 67.4 | 87.7 | 11,854 | 10 | 0 |
| DeepSeek effort 50 | **72.4** | 202.4 | 5,512 | 2 | 0 |
| DeepSeek effort 75 | 66.7 | 251.1 | 6,980 | 3 | 2 (see below) |

- **Main result: DeepSeek's better setting (effort 50) minus Qwen's better setting (medium): -16.1 points per item
  (95 % -31.0 to -3.4).** DeepSeek better on 1 item (c16 JSON parser, 1.0 vs 0.67, after 800 s), worse on 5 (four
  honesty items and m09), equal on 23. The upper bound (each model's best answer per item over all its settings) is
  also -16.1.
- Without the 8 honesty items the two are level: on the other 21, DeepSeek e50 is 0.67 items behind (-3.2 points per
  item; c16 +0.33, m09 -1).
- Honesty (8 made-up questions; full marks = says it does not know): Qwen medium 87.5, xhigh 12.5; DeepSeek e50 37.5,
  e75 25.0, **e25 37.5** - the same three items right as e50. Less effort does not help DeepSeek here: at e25 it still
  thought ~4,700 tokens (165 s) per item before inventing an answer. Qwen's drop with effort is steeper here than on
  10-17's quick set (100 / 96.7 / 73.3 low / medium / xhigh), but these 8 items were picked because Qwen had failed or
  wavered on them, so the two are not comparable.
- Blind research (Phase 4 method, /10; key opened after): r02 Qwen medium 6.0, xhigh 8.0, DeepSeek e50 5.5, e75 3.0;
  r05 8.5, 6.0, 7.5, 6.0. Means 7.25 / 7.0 / 6.5 / 4.5 - Qwen medium ahead of DeepSeek e50 on both.
- DeepSeek effort 75's two cut-offs are not the room. c02_rle fell into a loop ("0000...") that the server's
  one-token repeat guard ended at 4,500 tokens. r02 filled DeepSeek's 32K context: after 10 tool calls the conversation
  was 31,252 tokens, leaving 256 to answer. 128K context (section 10) would have avoided the second.
- m03 reached the thinking limit under all four settings and scored 0 everywhere: too hard for this room.
- Effort: DeepSeek e50 beats e75 (72.4 vs 66.7, 50 s less per answer). Qwen medium beats xhigh by 21 points, mostly on
  honesty and one instruction item. **Suggested DeepSeek default: effort 50** (Karl decides).
- DSpark: not loaded in any of the hard set's DeepSeek runs (`auto`), so no A/B/A ran and the config is unchanged.

## 10. Daily context 128K (10-27 night): PASS, switched

Karl (10-26 ~22:00): "set DeepSeek's daily context to 128K if a filled-context check passes." Details:
results/ds41/ctx128/summary.md.

**The long prompt** (the daily config at 131,072; everything up as in the daily memory check: SearXNG 8889 / 8890 and
a 5.17 GiB ballast standing in for LDR; soft stop at 14 GB): 96,917 tokens of the long speed text plus a question.

| read | decode at that length | first answer token | lowest MemAvailable | the slot it leaves |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 102.3 s (947 tok/s) | 21.0 tok/s (452 tokens) | 120 s | 18.9 GB (>= 14: PASS) | 97,405 tokens: 0.10 GB in RAM, 100.9 MB on disk |

- Follow-up on it: taken back from RAM, 115 new tokens read, first token in 3.6 s, decode 21.8 tok/s.
- Swap: one 11.9 MB swap-out burst of other programs' idle pages (the first, logged only); engine swap 0; kernel log
  clean.

**Worst case for 16 RAM slots.** A slot costs ~0.96 MiB per 1K tokens, so 0.126 GiB at 128K. Without slots the daily
memory check leaves 16.1 GB (15.5 GB with its 16 daily-length slots of 0.64 GB). 16 full 128K slots plus one being
written would leave 13.99 GB, just under the rule. So the RAM slots are capped at **1.7 GiB** (16.14 - 14 - 0.25
margin - one slot in flight): ~13 full-length 128K conversations or all 16 daily-length ones. Past the cap the least
recently used goes to the folder and comes back from it when continued.

**The cap** is a small local engine change ("slotram", two edits in ds4_main.cpp's slot_put: STRATA_DS4_SLOT_RAM_GB caps
the RAM slots' bytes as STRATA_DS4_SLOT_CAP caps their count; unset = stock). Checks:
- Cap test (0.03 GiB, four ~3K-token chats, then the first continued): the first went to the folder and came back,
  reading only its 26 new tokens. PASS.
- Same answers as the shipped engine: two seeded requests on fresh engines with placement frozen give identical text
  (264 and 154 tokens on both). A first try with the expert cache on differed. A control then showed the shipped engine
  doesn't repeat itself in that mode either (292 vs 513 tokens), so that check could not pass for any build.

**Switched 10-27 02:13**: DS41-daily.json at 128K on the slot-cap build with the 1.7 GiB cap; the launcher smoke test
on 8086 passed.

**Long-context items** (optional): stopped after 6 min by the run swap guard. The kernel pushed 34 MB of other
programs' idle pages to swap in two bursts while the ~95K-token documents streamed the CPU-lane experts (121 GB, in
place in the model files) through the page cache. Engine swap stayed 0, and the lowest MemAvailable was 23.3 GB. Only 1
of 6 answers came back (la_needles, 1.00; Qwen 1.00 too), so there is no comparison. In daily use a very long prompt
can push a few tens of MB of idle desktop pages to swap; the engine itself did not swap.

**Run notes.** Two driver slips cost a re-run each; neither changed a result:
- The hard set's driver skipped the repeat pairs ("build failed"): it looked for the test build's record in its own
  state.
- The 128K driver read the long run's lowest memory from a field the arm leaves empty and called it a fail. The value
  is now taken from the monitor's line (after.py monitor_lowest).

Measured and drafted with Claude Code.
